{
  "$schema": "https://docs.oasis-open.org/sarif/sarif/v2.1.0/errata01/os/schemas/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "kv-cli",
          "version": "0.14.0",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/kovallent/kv-cli",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "KV001",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "missing contract parameter"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A governed function that does not declare a parameter the contract requires. The function runs and passes its tests. In production it quietly uses whatever the author's machine assumed, because nothing passes it the target environment."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "# KV001: missing contract parameter\n\nSeverity: set per parameter in the contract. In the default contract,\n`target_environment` is an error and `dry_run` is a warning.\n\n## What it catches\n\nA governed function that does not declare a parameter the contract requires.\nThe function runs and passes its tests. In production it quietly uses\nwhatever the author's machine assumed, because nothing passes it the target\nenvironment.\n\n```python\ndef deploy_service(name):                 # KV001: no target_environment\n    ...\n```\n\n## How to fix it\n\nDeclare the parameter, then pass it through from the call site:\n\n```python\ndef deploy_service(name, target_environment: str = \"dev\", dry_run: bool = False):\n    ...\n```\n\n`kv-cli fix` inserts the parameter with the annotation and default the\ncontract sets. It goes before `**kwargs` and keeps a multi-line signature's\nlayout. `fix` changes only the signature, so you still have to pass the real\nvalue at each call site.\n\n## Which functions are governed\n\nA function is governed when it matches any `applies_to` rule:\n\n- `name_patterns`, e.g. `deploy*`, `run_*`, `*_job`;\n- `decorators`, e.g. `task`, `kovallent.task`;\n- `all_functions: true`, which governs every function.\n\nEach finding says which rule matched:\n`in scope: name matches applies_to.name_patterns \"deploy*\"`.\n\nA function is never governed when:\n\n- its name matches `exempt_name_patterns` (`_*` and `test_*` by default),\n  which is checked first;\n- a framework owns its signature, for example dbt's `def model(dbt, session)`\n  or Databricks' `@dlt.table`. Adding a parameter there would break the\n  pipeline. `kv-cli frameworks` lists every owned signature.\n\nAirflow's `@task` is governed but report-only. `fix` prints a `manual:` item\ninstead of inserting a default, because an inserted default would read\n\"dev\" in every environment while looking correct.\n\n## How to turn it off for a function\n\nThere is no per-line marker for KV001. Exempt the function by name instead:\n\n```yaml\napplies_to:\n  exempt_name_patterns: [\"_*\", \"test_*\", \"legacy_*\"]\n```\n\n## Contract keys\n\n`parameters`, `applies_to`, `frameworks`, `fix.insert_missing_parameters`\n",
                "markdown": "# KV001: missing contract parameter\n\nSeverity: set per parameter in the contract. In the default contract,\n`target_environment` is an error and `dry_run` is a warning.\n\n## What it catches\n\nA governed function that does not declare a parameter the contract requires.\nThe function runs and passes its tests. In production it quietly uses\nwhatever the author's machine assumed, because nothing passes it the target\nenvironment.\n\n```python\ndef deploy_service(name):                 # KV001: no target_environment\n    ...\n```\n\n## How to fix it\n\nDeclare the parameter, then pass it through from the call site:\n\n```python\ndef deploy_service(name, target_environment: str = \"dev\", dry_run: bool = False):\n    ...\n```\n\n`kv-cli fix` inserts the parameter with the annotation and default the\ncontract sets. It goes before `**kwargs` and keeps a multi-line signature's\nlayout. `fix` changes only the signature, so you still have to pass the real\nvalue at each call site.\n\n## Which functions are governed\n\nA function is governed when it matches any `applies_to` rule:\n\n- `name_patterns`, e.g. `deploy*`, `run_*`, `*_job`;\n- `decorators`, e.g. `task`, `kovallent.task`;\n- `all_functions: true`, which governs every function.\n\nEach finding says which rule matched:\n`in scope: name matches applies_to.name_patterns \"deploy*\"`.\n\nA function is never governed when:\n\n- its name matches `exempt_name_patterns` (`_*` and `test_*` by default),\n  which is checked first;\n- a framework owns its signature, for example dbt's `def model(dbt, session)`\n  or Databricks' `@dlt.table`. Adding a parameter there would break the\n  pipeline. `kv-cli frameworks` lists every owned signature.\n\nAirflow's `@task` is governed but report-only. `fix` prints a `manual:` item\ninstead of inserting a default, because an inserted default would read\n\"dev\" in every environment while looking correct.\n\n## How to turn it off for a function\n\nThere is no per-line marker for KV001. Exempt the function by name instead:\n\n```yaml\napplies_to:\n  exempt_name_patterns: [\"_*\", \"test_*\", \"legacy_*\"]\n```\n\n## Contract keys\n\n`parameters`, `applies_to`, `frameworks`, `fix.insert_missing_parameters`\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/kovallent/kv-cli/blob/v0.14.0/docs/rules/KV001.md"
            },
            {
              "id": "KV002",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "hardcoded credential"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A credential written into source, a notebook or a config file. kv-cli reports a literal when either of these rules matches."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "# KV002: hardcoded credential\n\nSeverity: always an error.\n\n## What it catches\n\nA credential written into source, a notebook or a config file. kv-cli reports\na literal when either of these rules matches.\n\n1. Its shape. A value pattern matches it under any name and anywhere in the\n   file, including comments, notebook outputs and split strings such as\n   `\"ghp_abc\" \"def\"`. The shapes cover AWS keys, GitHub, Slack, OpenAI,\n   Anthropic, Stripe and Google keys, JWTs, PEM private keys and URLs with a\n   password.\n2. Its name. It is assigned to, or compared with, a name that matches\n   `secrets.key_patterns`, such as `*password*`, `*token*` or `*api_key*`.\n\n```python\nDB_PASSWORD = \"s3cr3t-value-1234\"         # KV002: name rule\nif password == \"s3cr3t-value-1234\":       # KV002: comparison\n```\n\nThe output redacts the value, shown as `value \"s3…\" (<redacted>, 17 chars)`,\nso the CI log doesn't leak the credential.\n\n## What it ignores\n\nThese exceptions apply to the name rule only. A literal that matches a shape\nis always reported.\n\n- Names that refer to a credential without holding one: `secret_name`,\n  `token_url`, `credentials_path` (`secrets.exempt_key_patterns`).\n- Placeholders such as `changeme`, `your-api-key-here` and `<token>`\n  (`allow_values`, `placeholder_patterns`).\n- References such as `${VAR}`, `{{ env_var('X') }}` and f-string\n  substitutions. A template is judged by what it writes down, so a\n  hardcoded fallback is still reported: `{{ env_var('X', 's3cr3t') }}`,\n  `{{ 's3cr3t' }}`, or a `{% if %}` branch that is a literal.\n- Values shorter than `min_value_length`.\n\nNames are matched as written and in snake_case, so `privateKey` matches\n`*private_key*`.\n\n## How to fix it\n\nRead the value from the environment or a secret store:\n\n```python\nimport os\nDB_PASSWORD = os.environ[\"DB_PASSWORD\"]\n```\n\n`kv-cli fix` makes this rewrite and adds `import os` if it is missing. A\nhardcoded default in a function signature is left as a `manual:` item,\nbecause `os.environ[...]` in a default is read once, at import time.\n\nAfterwards, rotate the credential. A value that was ever committed is still\nin git history, whatever the file says now.\n\n## How to suppress one line\n\n```python\nlegacy_token = \"abcd1234efgh\"  # kovallent:allow-secret\n```\n\nThe marker counts only inside a comment. JSON has no comments, so exclude a\nfixture file with `scan.exclude` instead.\n\n## Contract keys\n\n`secrets.key_patterns`, `secrets.value_patterns`,\n`secrets.exempt_key_patterns`, `secrets.allow_values`,\n`secrets.placeholder_patterns`, `secrets.min_value_length`,\n`secrets.ignore_marker`, `fix.externalize_secrets`, `fix.env_var_prefix`\n",
                "markdown": "# KV002: hardcoded credential\n\nSeverity: always an error.\n\n## What it catches\n\nA credential written into source, a notebook or a config file. kv-cli reports\na literal when either of these rules matches.\n\n1. Its shape. A value pattern matches it under any name and anywhere in the\n   file, including comments, notebook outputs and split strings such as\n   `\"ghp_abc\" \"def\"`. The shapes cover AWS keys, GitHub, Slack, OpenAI,\n   Anthropic, Stripe and Google keys, JWTs, PEM private keys and URLs with a\n   password.\n2. Its name. It is assigned to, or compared with, a name that matches\n   `secrets.key_patterns`, such as `*password*`, `*token*` or `*api_key*`.\n\n```python\nDB_PASSWORD = \"s3cr3t-value-1234\"         # KV002: name rule\nif password == \"s3cr3t-value-1234\":       # KV002: comparison\n```\n\nThe output redacts the value, shown as `value \"s3…\" (<redacted>, 17 chars)`,\nso the CI log doesn't leak the credential.\n\n## What it ignores\n\nThese exceptions apply to the name rule only. A literal that matches a shape\nis always reported.\n\n- Names that refer to a credential without holding one: `secret_name`,\n  `token_url`, `credentials_path` (`secrets.exempt_key_patterns`).\n- Placeholders such as `changeme`, `your-api-key-here` and `<token>`\n  (`allow_values`, `placeholder_patterns`).\n- References such as `${VAR}`, `{{ env_var('X') }}` and f-string\n  substitutions. A template is judged by what it writes down, so a\n  hardcoded fallback is still reported: `{{ env_var('X', 's3cr3t') }}`,\n  `{{ 's3cr3t' }}`, or a `{% if %}` branch that is a literal.\n- Values shorter than `min_value_length`.\n\nNames are matched as written and in snake_case, so `privateKey` matches\n`*private_key*`.\n\n## How to fix it\n\nRead the value from the environment or a secret store:\n\n```python\nimport os\nDB_PASSWORD = os.environ[\"DB_PASSWORD\"]\n```\n\n`kv-cli fix` makes this rewrite and adds `import os` if it is missing. A\nhardcoded default in a function signature is left as a `manual:` item,\nbecause `os.environ[...]` in a default is read once, at import time.\n\nAfterwards, rotate the credential. A value that was ever committed is still\nin git history, whatever the file says now.\n\n## How to suppress one line\n\n```python\nlegacy_token = \"abcd1234efgh\"  # kovallent:allow-secret\n```\n\nThe marker counts only inside a comment. JSON has no comments, so exclude a\nfixture file with `scan.exclude` instead.\n\n## Contract keys\n\n`secrets.key_patterns`, `secrets.value_patterns`,\n`secrets.exempt_key_patterns`, `secrets.allow_values`,\n`secrets.placeholder_patterns`, `secrets.min_value_length`,\n`secrets.ignore_marker`, `fix.externalize_secrets`, `fix.env_var_prefix`\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/kovallent/kv-cli/blob/v0.14.0/docs/rules/KV002.md"
            },
            {
              "id": "KV003",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "pinned infrastructure identifier"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An identifier that ties code to one environment, such as a warehouse, catalog, bucket, cluster ID, workspace URL, broker or JDBC endpoint. The code works in that one environment and either breaks in the others or, worse, writes to the wrong one."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "# KV003: pinned infrastructure identifier\n\nSeverity: a warning by default, so turning it on doesn't break CI on day one.\nSet `infrastructure.severity: error` to gate on it, or run `audit --strict`.\n\n## What it catches\n\nAn identifier that ties code to one environment, such as a warehouse,\ncatalog, bucket, cluster ID, workspace URL, broker or JDBC endpoint. The\ncode works in that one environment and either breaks in the others or,\nworse, writes to the wrong one.\n\n```python\nfrom snowflake.snowpark import Session\n\nWAREHOUSE = \"ANALYTICS_PROD_WH\"           # KV003: name rule\ndf.write.parquet(\"s3://acme-prod-orders/\") # KV003: object-store URI\n```\n\nPython files, [Databricks asset bundles](#databricks-asset-bundles) and\n[dbt SQL models](#dbt-sql-models). Other config files, dbt's `profiles.yml`\nabove all, are where environment-specific values belong, so KV003 never\napplies to them.\n\nThe name rule, `infrastructure.key_patterns`, applies only in files that use\na data framework, that is, where a framework profile is detected. In a file\nthat imports none, a `warehouse` or `bucket` is as likely to be a CLI option\nor a test fixture as a deployment target. Value patterns, such as the\nobject-store URI above, apply in every Python file. To apply the name rule in\nevery Python file, as before 0.10.0, set\n`infrastructure.global_key_patterns_require_framework: false`.\n\nFramework profiles add their own identifiers in files that use the\nframework. For example, Snowpark adds `account` and `role`, and Airflow adds\n`conn_id`, `pool` and `queue`. `kv-cli frameworks` lists them all.\n\n## Databricks asset bundles\n\nA bundle declares what varies between targets, under `targets:` and\n`variables:`, so an identifier anywhere else in it is pinned by mistake.\nKV003 applies to `databricks.yml` (or `databricks.yaml`) and to the YAML\nfiles its `include:` globs name, such as `resources/*.yml`. A file belongs to\nthe nearest `databricks.yml` above it, up to the repository root. dbt's\n`profiles.yml` never counts, even when a bundle includes it.\n\n```yaml\nresources:\n  jobs:\n    nightly:\n      tasks:\n        - task_key: load\n          existing_cluster_id: 0923-164208-prdclstr # KV003\n          spark_python_task:\n            parameters:\n              - s3://prod-orders/raw/ # KV003\n        - task_key: report\n          sql_task:\n            warehouse_id: ${var.warehouse_id} # a substitution: not reported\n\ntargets:\n  dev:\n    resources:\n      jobs:\n        nightly:\n          tasks:\n            - task_key: load\n              existing_cluster_id: 0101-000000-devclstr # per target: not reported\n```\n\nA scalar is reported when all of these hold:\n\n- it isn't under `targets:` or `variables:` (a variable's `default` is a\n  declared default);\n- it isn't a substitution such as `${var.x}`, `${bundle.target}`,\n  `${workspace.host}` or `${resources.jobs.x.id}`;\n- its key matches `infrastructure.key_patterns` or the databricks profile's\n  infrastructure keys, such as `*warehouse_id*` and `*instance_pool*`, or its\n  value matches `infrastructure.value_patterns`, such as an `s3://` URI.\n\n`allow_values`, `exempt_key_patterns`, `min_value_length` and the\n`kovallent:allow-infra` marker work as they do in Python. The finding's\n`symbol` is the scalar's dotted path,\n`resources.jobs.nightly.tasks[0].existing_cluster_id`, so its fingerprint\nsurvives a moved line. A bundle file's entry in `run.files` reads\n`analysis: credentials_and_infrastructure`.\n\nFix it by moving the value under `targets:`, or into a variable used as\n`${var.<name>}`. Set `infrastructure.bundle_files: false` to make every YAML\nfile credentials-only again, as before 0.13.0.\n\n## dbt SQL models\n\nFrom 0.14.0, KV003 reads the `.sql` models of each dbt project in the scan.\nA model is a `.sql` file under the `model-paths` (`source-paths` before dbt\n1.0) or `snapshot-paths` of the nearest `dbt_project.yml` above it, up to the\nrepository root. `scan.include` doesn't need to list `.sql`. The project's\n`target-path`, `dbt_packages/` and `dbt_modules/` are never read, and\n`scan.exclude` still applies.\n\n```sql\nuse warehouse ANALYTICS_PROD_WH;                          -- KV003: use_warehouse\nselect o.order_id\nfrom ANALYTICS_PROD.RAW.ORDERS as o                       -- KV003: three_part_name\njoin {{ ref('stg_customers') }} as c on c.id = o.customer_id  -- a ref(): not reported\n```\n\nkv-cli doesn't render Jinja: it replaces each tag with text of the same\nlength, so lines stay exact, and parses the result with sqlparser-rs. A\n`ref()` or `source()` becomes a placeholder and is never reported. These are\nreported, each as its own `detector`:\n\n| `detector` | What | `subject` |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `three_part_name` | A literal `database.schema.table` relation, in `FROM` or `JOIN`, or the target of `INSERT`, `MERGE`, `UPDATE` or `DELETE` | The name, unquoted and lower-cased: `analytics_prod.raw.orders` |\n| `use_warehouse` | `USE WAREHOUSE x` | `x`, normalised |\n| `use_database` | `USE DATABASE x` | `x`, normalised |\n| `object_store_uri` | A string literal matching the `object_store_uri` value pattern, in a `COPY INTO … FROM` or `TO` clause, as the target of an unload (`COPY INTO '<uri>' FROM …`), or directly after `LOCATION` | The URI exactly as matched |\n| `two_part_name` | A literal `schema.table` relation, only with `sql.two_part_names: true` | The name, normalised |\n\nAn unload writes to its target, so a hardcoded path there is reported like\none it reads from:\n\n```sql\ncopy into 's3://acme-prod-exports/orders/'  -- KV003: object_store_uri\nfrom {{ ref('orders') }}\n```\n\nNot reported:\n\n- a name or string with Jinja in it, such as\n  `analytics_{{ target.name }}.raw.orders` or `'s3://{{ var(\"bucket\") }}/'`:\n  it varies by target. A tag that is one quoted string,\n  `{{ 'ANALYTICS_PROD.RAW.ORDERS' }}`, writes that string, so it is reported;\n- CTE names and aliases, and a two-part name whose first part is one, such as\n  BigQuery's `from orders o, o.items`;\n- table functions, such as `generate_series(…)` or `read_files(…)`;\n- names that `sql.allow_names` matches, ignoring case: by default anything\n  with an `information_schema` part, `pg_catalog.*`,\n  `snowflake.account_usage.*`, `snowflake.organization_usage.*` and\n  `bigquery-public-data.*`;\n- a value in `infrastructure.allow_values`, and a line whose comment carries\n  `-- kovallent:allow-infra`.\n\nThe finding's `symbol` is the model name, its file stem, and its `subject` is\nthe pinned thing, so two hardcoded tables in one model are two findings with\ntheir own fingerprints. The same thing written twice in one model is one\nfinding, at its first line, and its detail lists the others. `key` is the\nname as written; it is absent for a URI.\n\nA model that no SQL dialect parses, or whose Jinja can't be neutralised, is\nlisted in `skipped` as `sql_not_parsed`: a warning, which gates under\n`--strict`, like a Python syntax error. Its detail names the cause. The same\nrules still run over its tokens, and their findings say so. A model that\nparses reads `analysis: sql` in `run.files`; SQL adds nothing to the scope\nblock's function counts.\n\nStage 1 doesn't check:\n\n- names built or stored in Jinja, such as `{% set t = '…' %}` used as\n  `{{ t }}`;\n- hooks: `pre_hook`, `post_hook` and the hooks in `dbt_project.yml`;\n- `config()` arguments, such as `snowflake_warehouse='PROD_WH'`;\n- analyses and singular data tests;\n- `USE SCHEMA`, `USE ROLE` and JDBC URLs;\n- object-store paths outside `COPY` and `LOCATION`, such as\n  `read_parquet('gs://…')`.\n\nFix a relation with `{{ ref() }}` or `{{ source() }}`, a `USE` statement by\nsetting the warehouse or database on the dbt target in `profiles.yml`, and a\npath by passing it in with `var()` or `env_var()`. `kv-cli fix` lists each\nSQL finding as a `manual:` item and never rewrites SQL. Set\n`sql.enabled: false` to read no SQL, as before 0.14.0. See\n[Configuration](../configuration.md#sql) for the `sql:` section.\n\n## How to fix it\n\nDerive the value from the target environment:\n\n```python\nWAREHOUSES = {\"dev\": \"ANALYTICS_DEV_WH\", \"prod\": \"ANALYTICS_PROD_WH\"}\n\ndef run_report(target_environment: str = \"dev\"):\n    warehouse = WAREHOUSES[target_environment]\n```\n\n`kv-cli fix` never rewrites these, because the right replacement is a\ndeployment decision. In dbt SQL models it lists them as `manual:` items.\n\n## What it ignores\n\n- Values in `infrastructure.allow_values`, such as `local`, `dev` or `test`.\n- Names in `infrastructure.exempt_key_patterns`, such as `*_column` or\n  `*_type`.\n- Values shorter than `min_value_length`.\n- Comparisons such as `if catalog == \"prod\"`. Those branch on an environment\n  rather than pin one.\n\n## How to suppress one line\n\n```python\nLEGACY_BUCKET = \"s3://acme-archive/\"  # kovallent:allow-infra\n```\n\n## Contract keys\n\n`infrastructure.enabled`, `infrastructure.severity`,\n`infrastructure.key_patterns`,\n`infrastructure.global_key_patterns_require_framework`,\n`infrastructure.value_patterns`,\n`infrastructure.allow_values`, `infrastructure.exempt_key_patterns`,\n`infrastructure.ignore_marker`, `infrastructure.bundle_files`, `sql.enabled`,\n`sql.paths`, `sql.dialect`, `sql.allow_names`, `sql.two_part_names`\n",
                "markdown": "# KV003: pinned infrastructure identifier\n\nSeverity: a warning by default, so turning it on doesn't break CI on day one.\nSet `infrastructure.severity: error` to gate on it, or run `audit --strict`.\n\n## What it catches\n\nAn identifier that ties code to one environment, such as a warehouse,\ncatalog, bucket, cluster ID, workspace URL, broker or JDBC endpoint. The\ncode works in that one environment and either breaks in the others or,\nworse, writes to the wrong one.\n\n```python\nfrom snowflake.snowpark import Session\n\nWAREHOUSE = \"ANALYTICS_PROD_WH\"           # KV003: name rule\ndf.write.parquet(\"s3://acme-prod-orders/\") # KV003: object-store URI\n```\n\nPython files, [Databricks asset bundles](#databricks-asset-bundles) and\n[dbt SQL models](#dbt-sql-models). Other config files, dbt's `profiles.yml`\nabove all, are where environment-specific values belong, so KV003 never\napplies to them.\n\nThe name rule, `infrastructure.key_patterns`, applies only in files that use\na data framework, that is, where a framework profile is detected. In a file\nthat imports none, a `warehouse` or `bucket` is as likely to be a CLI option\nor a test fixture as a deployment target. Value patterns, such as the\nobject-store URI above, apply in every Python file. To apply the name rule in\nevery Python file, as before 0.10.0, set\n`infrastructure.global_key_patterns_require_framework: false`.\n\nFramework profiles add their own identifiers in files that use the\nframework. For example, Snowpark adds `account` and `role`, and Airflow adds\n`conn_id`, `pool` and `queue`. `kv-cli frameworks` lists them all.\n\n## Databricks asset bundles\n\nA bundle declares what varies between targets, under `targets:` and\n`variables:`, so an identifier anywhere else in it is pinned by mistake.\nKV003 applies to `databricks.yml` (or `databricks.yaml`) and to the YAML\nfiles its `include:` globs name, such as `resources/*.yml`. A file belongs to\nthe nearest `databricks.yml` above it, up to the repository root. dbt's\n`profiles.yml` never counts, even when a bundle includes it.\n\n```yaml\nresources:\n  jobs:\n    nightly:\n      tasks:\n        - task_key: load\n          existing_cluster_id: 0923-164208-prdclstr # KV003\n          spark_python_task:\n            parameters:\n              - s3://prod-orders/raw/ # KV003\n        - task_key: report\n          sql_task:\n            warehouse_id: ${var.warehouse_id} # a substitution: not reported\n\ntargets:\n  dev:\n    resources:\n      jobs:\n        nightly:\n          tasks:\n            - task_key: load\n              existing_cluster_id: 0101-000000-devclstr # per target: not reported\n```\n\nA scalar is reported when all of these hold:\n\n- it isn't under `targets:` or `variables:` (a variable's `default` is a\n  declared default);\n- it isn't a substitution such as `${var.x}`, `${bundle.target}`,\n  `${workspace.host}` or `${resources.jobs.x.id}`;\n- its key matches `infrastructure.key_patterns` or the databricks profile's\n  infrastructure keys, such as `*warehouse_id*` and `*instance_pool*`, or its\n  value matches `infrastructure.value_patterns`, such as an `s3://` URI.\n\n`allow_values`, `exempt_key_patterns`, `min_value_length` and the\n`kovallent:allow-infra` marker work as they do in Python. The finding's\n`symbol` is the scalar's dotted path,\n`resources.jobs.nightly.tasks[0].existing_cluster_id`, so its fingerprint\nsurvives a moved line. A bundle file's entry in `run.files` reads\n`analysis: credentials_and_infrastructure`.\n\nFix it by moving the value under `targets:`, or into a variable used as\n`${var.<name>}`. Set `infrastructure.bundle_files: false` to make every YAML\nfile credentials-only again, as before 0.13.0.\n\n## dbt SQL models\n\nFrom 0.14.0, KV003 reads the `.sql` models of each dbt project in the scan.\nA model is a `.sql` file under the `model-paths` (`source-paths` before dbt\n1.0) or `snapshot-paths` of the nearest `dbt_project.yml` above it, up to the\nrepository root. `scan.include` doesn't need to list `.sql`. The project's\n`target-path`, `dbt_packages/` and `dbt_modules/` are never read, and\n`scan.exclude` still applies.\n\n```sql\nuse warehouse ANALYTICS_PROD_WH;                          -- KV003: use_warehouse\nselect o.order_id\nfrom ANALYTICS_PROD.RAW.ORDERS as o                       -- KV003: three_part_name\njoin {{ ref('stg_customers') }} as c on c.id = o.customer_id  -- a ref(): not reported\n```\n\nkv-cli doesn't render Jinja: it replaces each tag with text of the same\nlength, so lines stay exact, and parses the result with sqlparser-rs. A\n`ref()` or `source()` becomes a placeholder and is never reported. These are\nreported, each as its own `detector`:\n\n| `detector` | What | `subject` |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `three_part_name` | A literal `database.schema.table` relation, in `FROM` or `JOIN`, or the target of `INSERT`, `MERGE`, `UPDATE` or `DELETE` | The name, unquoted and lower-cased: `analytics_prod.raw.orders` |\n| `use_warehouse` | `USE WAREHOUSE x` | `x`, normalised |\n| `use_database` | `USE DATABASE x` | `x`, normalised |\n| `object_store_uri` | A string literal matching the `object_store_uri` value pattern, in a `COPY INTO … FROM` or `TO` clause, as the target of an unload (`COPY INTO '<uri>' FROM …`), or directly after `LOCATION` | The URI exactly as matched |\n| `two_part_name` | A literal `schema.table` relation, only with `sql.two_part_names: true` | The name, normalised |\n\nAn unload writes to its target, so a hardcoded path there is reported like\none it reads from:\n\n```sql\ncopy into 's3://acme-prod-exports/orders/'  -- KV003: object_store_uri\nfrom {{ ref('orders') }}\n```\n\nNot reported:\n\n- a name or string with Jinja in it, such as\n  `analytics_{{ target.name }}.raw.orders` or `'s3://{{ var(\"bucket\") }}/'`:\n  it varies by target. A tag that is one quoted string,\n  `{{ 'ANALYTICS_PROD.RAW.ORDERS' }}`, writes that string, so it is reported;\n- CTE names and aliases, and a two-part name whose first part is one, such as\n  BigQuery's `from orders o, o.items`;\n- table functions, such as `generate_series(…)` or `read_files(…)`;\n- names that `sql.allow_names` matches, ignoring case: by default anything\n  with an `information_schema` part, `pg_catalog.*`,\n  `snowflake.account_usage.*`, `snowflake.organization_usage.*` and\n  `bigquery-public-data.*`;\n- a value in `infrastructure.allow_values`, and a line whose comment carries\n  `-- kovallent:allow-infra`.\n\nThe finding's `symbol` is the model name, its file stem, and its `subject` is\nthe pinned thing, so two hardcoded tables in one model are two findings with\ntheir own fingerprints. The same thing written twice in one model is one\nfinding, at its first line, and its detail lists the others. `key` is the\nname as written; it is absent for a URI.\n\nA model that no SQL dialect parses, or whose Jinja can't be neutralised, is\nlisted in `skipped` as `sql_not_parsed`: a warning, which gates under\n`--strict`, like a Python syntax error. Its detail names the cause. The same\nrules still run over its tokens, and their findings say so. A model that\nparses reads `analysis: sql` in `run.files`; SQL adds nothing to the scope\nblock's function counts.\n\nStage 1 doesn't check:\n\n- names built or stored in Jinja, such as `{% set t = '…' %}` used as\n  `{{ t }}`;\n- hooks: `pre_hook`, `post_hook` and the hooks in `dbt_project.yml`;\n- `config()` arguments, such as `snowflake_warehouse='PROD_WH'`;\n- analyses and singular data tests;\n- `USE SCHEMA`, `USE ROLE` and JDBC URLs;\n- object-store paths outside `COPY` and `LOCATION`, such as\n  `read_parquet('gs://…')`.\n\nFix a relation with `{{ ref() }}` or `{{ source() }}`, a `USE` statement by\nsetting the warehouse or database on the dbt target in `profiles.yml`, and a\npath by passing it in with `var()` or `env_var()`. `kv-cli fix` lists each\nSQL finding as a `manual:` item and never rewrites SQL. Set\n`sql.enabled: false` to read no SQL, as before 0.14.0. See\n[Configuration](../configuration.md#sql) for the `sql:` section.\n\n## How to fix it\n\nDerive the value from the target environment:\n\n```python\nWAREHOUSES = {\"dev\": \"ANALYTICS_DEV_WH\", \"prod\": \"ANALYTICS_PROD_WH\"}\n\ndef run_report(target_environment: str = \"dev\"):\n    warehouse = WAREHOUSES[target_environment]\n```\n\n`kv-cli fix` never rewrites these, because the right replacement is a\ndeployment decision. In dbt SQL models it lists them as `manual:` items.\n\n## What it ignores\n\n- Values in `infrastructure.allow_values`, such as `local`, `dev` or `test`.\n- Names in `infrastructure.exempt_key_patterns`, such as `*_column` or\n  `*_type`.\n- Values shorter than `min_value_length`.\n- Comparisons such as `if catalog == \"prod\"`. Those branch on an environment\n  rather than pin one.\n\n## How to suppress one line\n\n```python\nLEGACY_BUCKET = \"s3://acme-archive/\"  # kovallent:allow-infra\n```\n\n## Contract keys\n\n`infrastructure.enabled`, `infrastructure.severity`,\n`infrastructure.key_patterns`,\n`infrastructure.global_key_patterns_require_framework`,\n`infrastructure.value_patterns`,\n`infrastructure.allow_values`, `infrastructure.exempt_key_patterns`,\n`infrastructure.ignore_marker`, `infrastructure.bundle_files`, `sql.enabled`,\n`sql.paths`, `sql.dialect`, `sql.allow_names`, `sql.two_part_names`\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/kovallent/kv-cli/blob/v0.14.0/docs/rules/KV003.md"
            },
            {
              "id": "KV004",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "naming convention"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Python function whose name doesn't match a naming rule in the contract. There are no rules by default, so KV004 reports nothing until a contract adds one."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "# KV004: naming convention\n\nSeverity: `naming.severity`, a warning by default, so adding a rule doesn't\nbreak CI on day one. Run `audit --strict` to gate on it, or set\n`naming.severity: error`.\n\n## What it catches\n\nA Python function whose name doesn't match a naming rule in the contract.\nThere are no rules by default, so KV004 reports nothing until a contract adds\none.\n\n```yaml\nnaming:\n  severity: warning\n  rules:\n    - name: pipeline_functions\n      applies_to: in_scope\n      pattern: \"^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*$\"\n      message: \"pipeline entrypoints use snake_case\"\n```\n\n```python\n@task\ndef LoadOrders(target_environment: str = \"dev\", dry_run: bool = False):  # KV004\n    ...\n```\n\n```text\n    14  warning  KV004  `LoadOrders` does not match naming rule `pipeline_functions`\n        name must match `^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*$`: pipeline entrypoints use snake_case\n```\n\n`pattern` is a regex matched against the whole function name, as if it were\nwrapped in `^(?:...)$`. It must compile, and it must not match the empty\nstring: a pattern that accepts an empty name requires nothing of a name.\n`kv-cli contract check` reports either problem with its line.\n\nThe finding's `symbol` is the function name and its `subject` is the rule's\n`name`, so the fingerprint follows the same formula as every other finding\nand leaves out the line number.\n\n## Which functions are checked\n\n- `applies_to: in_scope`, the default, checks the functions the scope line\n  counts as in scope or framework-exempt. **Framework-exempt functions are\n  checked too.** A framework owns a function's signature, not its name, so a\n  dbt `def model(dbt, session)` still has a name worth checking.\n- `applies_to: all_functions` checks every function definition.\n\nEither way, a name that matches `applies_to.exempt_name_patterns` (`_*` and\n`test_*` by default) is never checked, so `_private` helpers and tests are\nleft alone.\n\nKV004 applies under every framework profile, because every one of them has\nPython function names. It checks Python files only.\n\n## What it does not count toward\n\nKV004 never counts toward a compliance rate. That rate is about the parameter\ncontract, KV001, and a naming finding doesn't change any count in the scope\nblock. `report_only` is a KV001 field, so it is absent on KV004 findings.\n\n## How to fix it\n\nRename the function, and every caller with it.\n\n`kv-cli fix` never renames a function, because a rename breaks every caller\nit can't see: other repositories, scheduler configs, dbt's own call to\n`model`. It lists each KV004 finding as a `manual:` item instead.\n\n## How to turn it off for a function\n\nThere is no per-line marker for KV004. Exempt the function by name, which also\ntakes it out of KV001:\n\n```yaml\napplies_to:\n  exempt_name_patterns: [\"_*\", \"test_*\", \"legacy_*\"]\n```\n\nOr narrow the rule: `applies_to: in_scope` leaves out functions the contract\ndoesn't govern.\n\n## Contract keys\n\n`naming.severity`, `naming.rules[].name`, `naming.rules[].applies_to`,\n`naming.rules[].pattern`, `naming.rules[].message`,\n`applies_to.exempt_name_patterns`\n",
                "markdown": "# KV004: naming convention\n\nSeverity: `naming.severity`, a warning by default, so adding a rule doesn't\nbreak CI on day one. Run `audit --strict` to gate on it, or set\n`naming.severity: error`.\n\n## What it catches\n\nA Python function whose name doesn't match a naming rule in the contract.\nThere are no rules by default, so KV004 reports nothing until a contract adds\none.\n\n```yaml\nnaming:\n  severity: warning\n  rules:\n    - name: pipeline_functions\n      applies_to: in_scope\n      pattern: \"^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*$\"\n      message: \"pipeline entrypoints use snake_case\"\n```\n\n```python\n@task\ndef LoadOrders(target_environment: str = \"dev\", dry_run: bool = False):  # KV004\n    ...\n```\n\n```text\n    14  warning  KV004  `LoadOrders` does not match naming rule `pipeline_functions`\n        name must match `^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*$`: pipeline entrypoints use snake_case\n```\n\n`pattern` is a regex matched against the whole function name, as if it were\nwrapped in `^(?:...)$`. It must compile, and it must not match the empty\nstring: a pattern that accepts an empty name requires nothing of a name.\n`kv-cli contract check` reports either problem with its line.\n\nThe finding's `symbol` is the function name and its `subject` is the rule's\n`name`, so the fingerprint follows the same formula as every other finding\nand leaves out the line number.\n\n## Which functions are checked\n\n- `applies_to: in_scope`, the default, checks the functions the scope line\n  counts as in scope or framework-exempt. **Framework-exempt functions are\n  checked too.** A framework owns a function's signature, not its name, so a\n  dbt `def model(dbt, session)` still has a name worth checking.\n- `applies_to: all_functions` checks every function definition.\n\nEither way, a name that matches `applies_to.exempt_name_patterns` (`_*` and\n`test_*` by default) is never checked, so `_private` helpers and tests are\nleft alone.\n\nKV004 applies under every framework profile, because every one of them has\nPython function names. It checks Python files only.\n\n## What it does not count toward\n\nKV004 never counts toward a compliance rate. That rate is about the parameter\ncontract, KV001, and a naming finding doesn't change any count in the scope\nblock. `report_only` is a KV001 field, so it is absent on KV004 findings.\n\n## How to fix it\n\nRename the function, and every caller with it.\n\n`kv-cli fix` never renames a function, because a rename breaks every caller\nit can't see: other repositories, scheduler configs, dbt's own call to\n`model`. It lists each KV004 finding as a `manual:` item instead.\n\n## How to turn it off for a function\n\nThere is no per-line marker for KV004. Exempt the function by name, which also\ntakes it out of KV001:\n\n```yaml\napplies_to:\n  exempt_name_patterns: [\"_*\", \"test_*\", \"legacy_*\"]\n```\n\nOr narrow the rule: `applies_to: in_scope` leaves out functions the contract\ndoesn't govern.\n\n## Contract keys\n\n`naming.severity`, `naming.rules[].name`, `naming.rules[].applies_to`,\n`naming.rules[].pattern`, `naming.rules[].message`,\n`applies_to.exempt_name_patterns`\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/kovallent/kv-cli/blob/v0.14.0/docs/rules/KV004.md"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
